WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.559
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.408
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:16.076
Unit twenty-one: Pricing Strategy. Today you will compare prices precisely with far / much + comparatives.

4
00:00:16.376 --> 00:00:19.074
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.301
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:24.965
Pricing.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.006
Pricing means the process of deciding how much to charge.

8
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:32.626
Smart pricing doubled the café's afternoon sales.

9
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:35.509
Your turn. Say: Pricing.

10
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:39.205
Pricing.

11
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:40.823
Premium.

12
00:00:41.817 --> 00:00:45.999
Premium means of higher price and quality; an extra amount paid.

13
00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:49.558
Buyers pay a premium for organic Kampot pepper.

14
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.733
Your turn. Say: Premium.

15
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:56.475
Premium.

16
00:00:57.217 --> 00:00:58.171
Discount.

17
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:01.821
Discount means a reduction in the usual price.

18
00:01:02.417 --> 00:01:06.007
A 10% discount attracted many first-time customers.

19
00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:08.921
Your turn. Say: Discount.

20
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:12.756
Discount.

21
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:14.402
Value.

22
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:18.032
Value means what a product is worth to the customer.

23
00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:21.580
Customers judge value, not just price.

24
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:24.603
Your turn. Say: Value.

25
00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:28.189
Value.

26
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.951
Demand.

27
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.122
Demand means how much of a product customers want to buy.

28
00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:36.995
Demand for fans soars in April.

29
00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:39.806
Your turn. Say: Demand.

30
00:01:42.667 --> 00:01:43.610
Demand.

31
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:45.467
Competitor.

32
00:01:46.483 --> 00:01:49.516
Competitor means a business selling similar products.

33
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:52.569
Our main competitor lowered its prices again.

34
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.650
Your turn. Say: Competitor.

35
00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.582
Competitor.

36
00:02:00.317 --> 00:02:01.132
Margin.

37
00:02:02.117 --> 00:02:05.602
Margin means the difference between cost and selling price.

38
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:09.424
Premium products usually carry a much higher margin.

39
00:02:10.017 --> 00:02:12.634
Your turn. Say: Margin.

40
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.292
Margin.

41
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.202
Psychology.

42
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:22.807
Psychology means the study of how the mind influences behaviour.

43
00:02:23.417 --> 00:02:28.644
Pricing psychology explains why $4.99 feels cheaper than $5.

44
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.076
Your turn. Say: Psychology.

45
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:36.062
Psychology.

46
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:40.541
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.383 --> 00:02:50.633
Visal, two bakeries in Battambang sell almost identical cakes. One charges $3, the other $8. Why does the expensive one sell out?

48
00:02:51.717 --> 00:03:01.765
Because its pricing signals quality. The $8 cake is far more expensive, but the shop, packaging and service feel much more special.

49
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.442
Isn't the cheaper bakery offering better value?

50
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:16.238
Value is what customers perceive. For gifts, people often want something that looks considerably more impressive than an everyday cake.

51
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.640
So should the cheap bakery raise its price?

52
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:29.045
Only if it improves the experience. Otherwise, a higher price simply makes it much less competitive.

53
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.107
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.308
Grammar focus: Comparatives with far and much.

55
00:03:36.608 --> 00:03:37.818
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:42.026
A premium price is far higher than a discount price.

57
00:03:42.917 --> 00:03:46.356
The $8 cake feels much more special than the $3 one.

58
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:50.631
Online prices are slightly lower than in-store prices.

59
00:03:51.567 --> 00:04:02.021
Use far, much, a lot, considerably for big differences and slightly, a little, marginally for small ones. 'Very' cannot modify comparatives: much cheaper, not 'very cheaper'.

60
00:04:02.783 --> 00:04:06.086
Be careful! Don't say: This is very more expensive.

61
00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:10.648
Say: This is much more expensive.

62
00:04:10.948 --> 00:04:13.946
Use much / far, not very, with comparatives.

63
00:04:14.733 --> 00:04:17.084
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.412
The Price Is a Message.

65
00:04:19.712 --> 00:04:23.906
Pricing is one of the most powerful yet least understood marketing decisions.

66
00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:32.159
Cost-plus pricing simply adds a margin to production costs, whereas value-based pricing asks what customers believe a product is worth.

67
00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:39.764
A premium price can make a product seem far more desirable, because many buyers use price as a signal of quality.

68
00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:48.490
Psychological pricing exploits the fact that $9.99 feels much cheaper than $10, even though the difference is only one cent.

69
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:57.200
Constant discounting is risky, since customers quickly learn to wait for sales and begin to see the brand as considerably less valuable.

70
00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:02.232
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.083 --> 00:05:05.454
This phone is blank cheaper than that one.

72
00:05:05.754 --> 00:05:08.723
A, very. B, much. C, most.

73
00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:14.069
The answer is B: much.

74
00:05:15.217 --> 00:05:17.611
A reduction in the usual price is a blank.

75
00:05:17.911 --> 00:05:21.344
A, premium. B, margin. C, discount.

76
00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.120
The answer is C: discount.

77
00:05:28.283 --> 00:05:31.258
The difference between cost and selling price is the blank.

78
00:05:31.558 --> 00:05:34.945
A, margin. B, demand. C, value.

79
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.372
The answer is A: margin.

80
00:05:41.517 --> 00:05:43.342
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:43.642 --> 00:05:49.826
A, Rice is far more cheaper here.. B, Rice is far cheaper here.. C, Rice is very cheaper here..

82
00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:56.249
The answer is B: Rice is far cheaper here..

83
00:05:57.383 --> 00:06:01.252
According to the reading, constant discounting can make customers…

84
00:06:01.552 --> 00:06:06.239
A, pay more. B, wait for sales. C, trust the brand more.

85
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.287
The answer is B: wait for sales.

86
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.965
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.817 --> 00:06:21.012
Why do so many prices end in .99?

88
00:06:37.817 --> 00:06:39.212
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:39.512 --> 00:07:23.005
Prices end in .99 because of a psychological effect called left-digit bias. When we see $9.99, our brain focuses on the first digit, so it feels much closer to $9 than to $10, even though the difference is only one cent. This makes the product seem slightly cheaper and more attractive. Shops also use .99 to suggest a bargain, whereas round numbers such as $10 can suggest premium quality. That is why luxury brands often avoid .99 endings altogether.

90
00:07:23.305 --> 00:07:25.386
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:26.283 --> 00:07:30.689
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and comparatives with far and much.

92
00:07:30.989 --> 00:07:35.325
Today's value is fairness. Look for solutions that are fair to everyone.

93
00:07:35.625 --> 00:07:38.428
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:38.728 --> 00:07:41.531
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

